WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
JENNY: Hi.

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.210
I'm Jenny, and today
I'll be talking to you

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.640
about the top
security threats APIs

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.260
face as well as best practices
for how to secure your APIs.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.040
Let's get started.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.222
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.000
API security is a broad field
with many vulnerabilities,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.220
threats, and
protection practices

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.320
to take into consideration.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.070
APIs face the same threats
as software and web apps,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.890
but because they have a
unique set of vulnerabilities,

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.470
they also face unique threats.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.860
We've boiled all these down
to six main security threats

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.080
that you should
protect against--

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.060
basic software bugs, injection
threats, service availability

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.620
threats, user identity
threats, insider threats,

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.050
and spam and abuse.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.950
The first API security threat
is your basic software bug.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.080
Protecting against
basic software bugs

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.410
is a matter of organizing
regular tests and reviews,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.690
and this is vital
to your success.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.940
Start by ensuring
your organization

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.580
has good software
engineering in place.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.670
This means using standardized
coding guidelines

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.040
and instituting code reviews.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.560
Ensure that new code
is tested regularly

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.890
by reproducing security issues
in your own environment,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.130
fixing them, and quickly
rolling them into production.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.700
You haven't already,
implement security reviews.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.710
Doing so ensures
that the security

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.940
issues that one API faces will
not be repeated or resurface

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.740
with another API.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.580
You may also consider engaging
in 3rd party penetration tests

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.760
in order to get a realistic
picture of how vulnerable

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.160
your systems, data, and APIs
are from an outside perspective.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.100
APIs also face
injection attack threats

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.300
like SQL injection, remote
code execution, or cross site

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.470
scripting.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.020
Injection attacks occur
when malicious code

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.350
is injected into a
vulnerable software program.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.789
Luckily, since APIs have open
API specifications or swagger

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:12.210
specs with well identified
inputs and outputs,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.490
these threats are easy
to protect against using

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.890
consistent input validation.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.440
API management
platforms like Apigee

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.010
provide a host of policies
that allow you to easily deploy

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.900
input validation.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.230
Service availability threats
are also a large concern

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.760
to organizations.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.910
These attacks target APIs
by flooding your network

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.690
with requests, resulting in
denial of service issues.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.710
To protect against
these attacks,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.220
we recommend that you use
the volumetric defense

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:44.580
tools Apigee provides, such
as rate limiting and quota

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.170
policies.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.600
Using these with other
Google Cloud tools

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.940
like Google Frontend
Load Balancers, which

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.910
are built specifically to
deal with DDoS protection,

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.760
is a great best
practice to follow.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.940
Although many people consider
good customer experience

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.790
as supporting and guiding a
customer through their purchase

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.070
journey, great
customer experience

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.040
also means protecting the
user and their identity

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.110
at every moment in time.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.630
To protect against
user identity threats,

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.880
you'll want to use
an API gateway that

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.350
allows you to integrate OAuth,
JSON Web Tokens, signatures,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.280
API keys, and more defense
mechanisms that validate

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.150
and ensure authentication
and authorization.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.520
With API management,
you don't need

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.940
to sacrifice user
satisfaction for security.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.130
Building and deploying
a developer portal

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.220
is a great way to give
your API consumers

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.950
a self-service
experience to understand

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:45.600
the APIs you're exposing and
request the access they need.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.500
Plus, Apigee offers API
monitoring and analytics

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.980
dashboards so that you can gain
a holistic view of the health

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.520
and security of
your API programs.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.980
Insider threats are
an increasing problem

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.620
for enterprise
security and remind us

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.980
that security breaches don't
just come from the outside in,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.150
but can also come from within.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.470
With so many employees and
contractors having access

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.380
to sensitive information
in an organization,

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.560
a key first step to defend
against insider threats

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.660
is to integrate
your API management

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.120
system and your
Google Cloud console

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.150
with your enterprise
single sign on system.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.610
You also want to
centralize and structure

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.760
your change management
processes so that developers

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.310
have clear visibility
of any changes that

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.290
are occurring to your
software infrastructure.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.610
This includes knowing who
is making the changes, what

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.370
the changes are,
who approved them,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.670
and why the changes
are being made.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.660
Last but not least, let's
talk about spam and abuse.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.180
A considerable
amount of API traffic

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.550
comes from automated
actors that engage

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:57.810
in credential stuffing, brute
forcing, and content scraping.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.910
The most important
thing to remember

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.760
is that you can't secure
your APIs against threats

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.410
that you can't see.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.130
Establish API traffic visibility
through an API management

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.320
platform like
Apigee, where you'll

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.510
be able to observe
and capitalize

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.630
upon good, legitimate
traffic, while also

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.000
being able to identify and
block against bad actors.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.480
In summary, APIs face
several security threats

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.650
ranging from basic software
bugs to spam and abuse.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.430
API management is a key part
of your security architecture

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.560
and will help you spend
less time reacting

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.130
to security issues and
more time innovating.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.740
Assessing opportunities
for improvement

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.930
and prioritizing
security efforts

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.720
based on perceived risks
is an important part

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.960
of doing business in today's
increasingly digital world,

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:51.200
and Google Cloud is here to
make your security defenses

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.710
intuitive, intelligent,
and reliable.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.440
To learn more, please
review the resources below.

